# Jira ticket — template

> The contract lives in [`specs/jira-ticket-standard.yaml`](../specs/jira-ticket-standard.yaml).
> This file is the human-readable face of it. If the two ever disagree, the spec
> wins and this file is the bug.
>
> Headings below are **Spanish because GPLO tickets are Spanish** — a form speaks
> the language of whoever fills it in. They are data, matched case- and
> accent-insensitively, tolerant of `2.` prefixes and of a trailing
> `(parenthetical)`.
>
> **`<<...>>` placeholders are sentinels.** They teach by example AND they are a
> contract: a ticket submitted with a sentinel still in it is REJECTED. Never
> "fill them in later".
>
> Do not use checkboxes, tables, or emoji in headings. Jira stores descriptions
> as ADF: `- [ ]` becomes an interactive taskList whose UI state silently drifts
> from the text, tables are painful to edit in place, and an emoji breaks
> heading matching.

## Feature / Big Improvement · Small Improvement

```markdown
## Contexto / Problema

<<What is broken or missing today, and what happens if nobody fixes it. Name the
evidence: a file:line, a measurement, a ticket. Not "it would be nice if".>>

## Alcance / Entregables

<<What actually ships. Name the artefacts — paths, endpoints, jobs. If something
is deliberately NOT in scope, say so here; that sentence prevents an argument
later.>>

## Plan de prueba (A/B/C)

- **A — baseline / happy path**: <<the thing works end to end. How will you know
  — what exact output or state do you expect?>>
- **B — control negativo**: <<the case that must FAIL. Name the exact assertion
  you invert and what error you expect. This is the section that proves the gate
  bites; a green A proves only that you did not test.>>
- **C — regresión**: <<what must keep working. Name an existing test path or id,
  or write `ninguno aún — <por qué>`.>>

## Métricas (métrica → tipo)

- <<what you will measure>> → <<cobertura / compliance>>
- <<a second one, if the work has a second dimension>> → <<calidad / exactitud>>
```

## Bug

A bug's natural shape is not A/B/C — forcing one makes you invent a "baseline"
for behaviour that is simply broken. The regression test *is* the C, which is
why it survives here.

```markdown
## Contexto / Problema

<<What the bug costs. Who hits it, how often, and what they see.>>

## Repro

<<Exact steps, from a known starting state. A bug nobody can reproduce is a
rumour. Include the environment if it matters.>>

## Esperado vs Actual

- **Esperado**: <<what should happen>>
- **Actual**: <<what happens instead — the real message, code or state, not
  "it breaks">>

## Test de regresión

<<An existing test path or id that will cover this once fixed, or
`ninguno aún — <por qué>`.>>

## Métricas (métrica → tipo)

- <<how you will know it is fixed and stays fixed>> → <<calidad / exactitud>>
```

## Subtask · Epic

Both carry `Contexto / Problema` and `Alcance / Entregables` only. A subtask
inherits its parent's test plan; an epic does not have one, its children do.

## The metric types — closed list

Five, and only five. Anything else is rejected. The point of a closed list is
that "número de cosas" stops being an acceptable answer to "what kind of
measurement is this?".

| Type | It answers |
|---|---|
| `cobertura / compliance` | What fraction of X satisfies Y |
| `calidad / exactitud` | How often the thing is RIGHT — false positives, accuracy |
| `tendencia / burn-down` | A count moving over time, in the direction you claimed |
| `rendimiento / eficiencia` | Time, throughput, resource use |
| `coste` | Money, tokens, CI minutes |

## How this gets checked

Presence of a heading proves nothing, so the checker also looks at what is
under it:

- **Sentinels** — any surviving `<<...>>` fails.
- **Metric lines** — each must parse as `- <metric> → <type>` with the type in
  the closed list. `->` is normalised to `→`.
- **B is not boilerplate** — "verificar que no funciona" and its cousins are
  rejected by name. B must say which assertion flips.
- **C names a referent** — a test path/id, or an explicit `ninguno aún — <por qué>`.
- **Escape hatch** — `N/A — <reason>` is valid **per section**, never for a whole
  ticket, and its usage rate is reported. A high rate means this template does
  not fit the work; that is information about the template, not about you.

Run `python .ai-playbook/scripts/rules/jira-ticket-standard.rule.py explain <KEY>`
to see, section by section, what matched.
